<p>US Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth asked General Randy George, the Army’s top uniformed officer, to step down effective immediately. The Pentagon said George would retire from his post as the 41st chief of staff of the Army, but it did not publicly give a reason. The move came while the United States was still actively at war with Iran, with some reports saying the conflict was about five weeks in and that U.S. forces were continuing to build up in the Middle East. George had served in the role since August or September 2023, after President Joe Biden nominated him and the Senate confirmed him, and his term had been expected to run until 2027. Reports cited in the articles said Hegseth wanted Army leadership more closely aligned with President Trump’s vision and his own priorities for the service, making the dismissal part of a broader Pentagon leadership reshuffle.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ZFmmdukqn8QzEg6u98qjPN-as6O62QZii0vHOJ-MH0gAnp77v9QqXdlB46uWYpqrvWsDsr9ZFn_Fg_Ypze5gC51cEntDdeOrZICKbQuVBUvod5WlOyvUYmf3YMbMX0Zk9yi6Zt-lk-B5z_d43NM8mkneLYht5-r7o_cLBDkvYCG-GH5mnqo2U5rhJ4Dw6wPyjg8SFd-lfKnRa7yc1iKIVVhlssh6QM2Pg_SEx-Bto7ycQCA5bsf6fNbwFfL_mKMMaHJTjkyk27X8E-MaOw' alt='FILE - Army Chief of Staff Gen. Randy George speaks during the POW/MIA National Recognition Day Ceremony at the Pentagon, Sept. 19, 2025, in Washington.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>The same shake-up also pushed out General David Hodne, who led the Army’s Transformation and Training Command, and Major General William Green, chief of the Army Chaplain Corps.</li><li>Dawn said U.S. outlets described George’s ouster as one of the most consequential Pentagon leadership upheavals in decades.</li><li>According to Dawn, The Washington Post first disclosed the removals, and CBS News and other organizations later confirmed them.</li></ul><blockquote>Will be retiring from his position as the 41st Chief of Staff of the Army effective immediately - Sean Parnell</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</